================================================================================
TEST T44990 -- ACL: Named Hardware on port - copy-to-mirror IP
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-09 (times NZ, box local)
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2, u3, u5; Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWP-8657, "ACL: Named Hardware on port - copy-to-mirror IP", 5 steps
Build:    swi_c  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (netboots), Marvell 98CX8514 Falcon
          swi_b  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (AT-x960-28YSQ, console u3)
Configs:  swi_c.cfg, swi_b.cfg (running-config after setup)

VERDICT: PASS -- a named hardware ACL with the copy-to-mirror action, bound to a port,
         copies matching frames to the ACL-driven mirror destination AND still
         forwards them, and ACL processing stops at that match.
      copied to the mirror destination  yes        (6 frames captured at tb277 eth3,
                                                    = its 6 ACL hits exactly)
      still forwarded to the far side . yes        (0% packet loss on the same pings)
      only the named direction copied . yes        (echo REQUESTS only, never REPLIES)
      destination captures nothing
        before the rule exists ........ confirmed  (baseline capture: 0 and 0)
      first-match termination ......... proven     (a deny AFTER it is never evaluated)
      control for that ................ proven     (the same deny BEFORE it bites, and
                                                    suppresses the mirroring too)
      list edited while bound ......... applies    (three edits, each took effect)
CAVEAT:  All mirror evidence in this log is frame-level, captured at tb277 eth3. Mirror
         status output is NOT used as proof here: on this platform `show mirror` cannot
         be read as a statement about the dataplane, so it is quoted only to record
         what the switch reported. The mirror destination port was bounced (shutdown /
         no shutdown) before any measurement, so that an absence of captured frames
         could not be an artefact of the destination port rather than of the ACL. The
         traffic was ICMP echo matched by an `ip` rule from one source to one
         destination, ingress on port1.0.48 only; no other protocol, address form or
         direction was tried, and the frames captured were counted, not decoded beyond
         echo request versus echo reply.

