STEP 1 -- make port1.0.10 an ACL-driven mirror destination                PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#mirror interface none
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Monitored Port Name: none (via ACL)       <-- the ACL-driven form; this is the
                                                mechanism copy-to-mirror targets, not
                                                an "off" switch
Then the destination port was bounced, so that a later absence of frames could not be
attributed to the destination port rather than to the ACL:
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10 ; shutdown ; no shutdown
  => STEP 1 PASS: the destination is configured as ACL-driven and has been bounced, so
     the measurements that follow are about the copy-to-mirror action alone.

STEP 2 -- baseline: no copy-to-mirror rule yet                            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
host# ./cap.sh t44990-d0
  eth3: echo REQUESTS = 0 ; echo REPLIES = 0 ; ping 6/6, 0% packet loss
                                            <-- the destination captures nothing while
                                                the traffic itself flows normally
  => STEP 2 PASS: traffic flows and the mirror destination captures nothing. Anything
     captured later is attributable to the copy-to-mirror rule.

STEP 3 -- "apply copy-to-mirror on a port and confirm the action"         PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: copy-to-mirror is defined as "copy to the mirror port AND forward". Proving
  only the mirror half would not distinguish it from send-to-mirror, which does not
  forward, so both halves are measured. All mirror evidence is frame-level, captured
  at tb277 eth3, because on this platform the mirror status output cannot be read as a
  statement about the dataplane.
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44990
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#copy-to-mirror ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; access-group t44990
x960-56YSQ#show access-list t44990
  Hardware IP access list t44990
      4 copy-to-mirror ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48
   access-group t44990

host# ./cap.sh t44990-a
  eth3: echo REQUESTS = 6 ; echo REPLIES = 0      <-- HALF ONE: the matching frames arrived
  ping: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss   <-- HALF TWO: still forwarded
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters t44990
      Packet Hits: 6                        <-- exactly the 6 frames captured
  => STEP 3 PASS: matching frames were copied to the mirror destination AND were still
     forwarded to the far side. The agreement between the ACL hit count and the
     captured frame count is what makes the mirror half positive proof. Only echo
     REQUESTS appear, never replies, which is correct: the rule matches src .65 -> dst
     .70, the request direction only -- so the capture also demonstrates that the rule
     selects rather than copying everything on the port.

STEP 4 -- copy-to-mirror TERMINATES ACL processing at the first match     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: one pair of rules, tested twice with only their ORDER changed, so that the
  two outcomes differ in nothing else.

  A) deny placed AFTER the copy-to-mirror:
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#8 deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show access-list t44990
      4 copy-to-mirror ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
      8 deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
host# ping -c 4 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70
  0% packet loss                            <-- the deny was NEVER EVALUATED

  B) CONTROL -- the same deny placed BEFORE the copy-to-mirror:
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#no 8
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#1 deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show access-list t44990
      1 deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
      4 copy-to-mirror ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
host# ping -c 4 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70
  100% packet loss                          <-- now the deny bites
host# ./cap.sh t44990-denyfirst
  eth3: echo REQUESTS = 0 ; echo REPLIES = 0  <-- and NOTHING is mirrored either

  => STEP 4 PASS: the identical two rules, with only their order changed, give opposite
     outcomes on BOTH the forwarding and the mirroring. With copy-to-mirror first, the
     later deny is never reached and traffic flows. With the deny first, the
     copy-to-mirror is never reached and nothing is mirrored. Processing stops at the
     first match -- exactly as documented -- and the control rules out "the deny simply
     does not work".

STEP 5 -- the named list was edited WHILE BOUND, three times              PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every edit in STEP 4 was made with `access-group t44990` still on port1.0.48. Each
produced:
  % This access-list is attached to an interface. The modified configuration will be applied
    to the interface automatically after exiting this configuration mode by entering "exit"
    or "end" command.
                                            <-- an ADVISORY about when the change is
                                                applied, not a refusal
and each edit DID take effect, as the opposite ping and capture results in STEP 4 show.
  => STEP 5 PASS: a named hardware ACL attached to an interface accepts edits in place.
     Three successive edits to the bound list each changed the dataplane outcome, which
     is positive proof that the `%` line is advisory and the modified list was applied
     to the interface.
